{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.FaddeevQuantumDilogarithm","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Analytic.IsolatedZeros","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Analytic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.periodic_binv_of_continuous","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.periodic_binv_of_continuous","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L161-L177","doc":"The modular-dual half, same argument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.periodic_b_of_continuous","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.periodic_b_of_continuous","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L138-L159","doc":"**Given that the ratio is entire, `ib`-periodicity holds EVERYWHERE.**\n\nThe algebra gives it off the prefactor zeros; those are nowhere dense; a\ncontinuous function is determined by its values on a dense set. So the open input\n`hibinv` (bean qou-rp5f) is not independent of `hratio` (bean qou-cons) — it\nfollows from it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.dense_compl_prefactor_binv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.dense_compl_prefactor_binv","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L128-L134","doc":"The dual prefactor `1 + e^{2πz/b}` likewise. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.dense_compl_prefactor_b","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.dense_compl_prefactor_b","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L119-L126","doc":"The compact prefactor `1 + e^{2πbz}` has a nowhere-dense zero set: it takes\nthe value `2` at `z = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.dense_compl_zeros","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.dense_compl_zeros","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L102-L117","doc":"An entire `g` that is nonzero somewhere has a zero set with DENSE COMPLEMENT.\n\nIdentity theorem: if the zero set had interior, `g` would vanish on a\nneighbourhood, hence identically on the (preconnected) plane. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_periodic_binv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_periodic_binv","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L91-L98","doc":"Period form of `ratio_shift_binv`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_periodic_b","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_periodic_b","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L80-L89","doc":"Period form of `ratio_shift_b`: the ratio is `ib`-periodic off the prefactor\nzeros. (Re-centred at `w = z + ib/2`, so the two shifted arguments become `w` and\n`w - ib`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_shift_binv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_shift_binv","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L72-L78","doc":"The modular-dual half. The guard is already in the `FaddeevPhi` axiom. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_shift_b","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.FaddeevRatioPeriodic.ratio_shift_b","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/FaddeevRatioPeriodic.lean#L59-L70","doc":"The ratio of two `FaddeevPhi b` solutions is invariant under the compact\n`ib`-shift wherever the shared prefactor is nonzero.\n\nThe whole content is that both shift equations carry the SAME prefactor, so it\ncancels from the ratio. Note this needs nothing about the zeros of `Φ`: at a\npoint where `Φ` vanishes both sides are `0`, and `mul_div_mul_left` covers it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}